    "passage": "The platypus is an egg-laying mammal endemic to Eastern Australia, [[1]] of Tasmania. It has brown fur all over its body and a wide, flat tail like a beaver’s. The platypus’ nose looks like a duck’s bill, but its nose can [[2]] many things that other animals’ noses cannot. With electroreception, the platypus can feel the power of other animals and keep its eyes [[3]] while swimming and hunting, using only its hearing and touch. Owing to its unusual appearance, the platypus was mistakenly [[4]] a fake by the first European scientists in the late eighteenth century, who encountered it for the first time and thought it was made of several animals sewn together. Furthermore, platypuses are difficult to see in the wild since they don’t like areas with people. [[5]] the number of platypuses is declining, they are not under any immediate threat.",
